{"id": "00000790-0000-4000-8000-000000000790", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This is a broad exploratory survey across many services rather than a focused root-cause hunt, so it's token-inefficient to do myself — better to delegate across the services in parallel to catch silent failures thoroughly.

I'll split into three sub-agents: one covering Lambda inventory and metrics (errors, throttles, DLQs, async destinations, event source mappings), one covering queues/streams/events (SQS DLQ depth, EventBridge failures, SNS delivery issues, Step Functions failed executions, Kinesis/DynamoDB streams), and one covering alarm coverage gaps (alarms in alarm state or missing actions, API Gateway 4xx/5xx, DynamoDB throttles) for account 123456789012. I'll keep it at default depth rather than deep mode, preload the metrics-exploration and log-searching skills, and draft the sub-agent prompts before calling tools.

"}]}

{"id": "000007b2-0000-4000-8000-0000000007b2", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The slsreview-* functions were only created about two hours ago, so I should check their async configs, DLQs, and URL configs, then pull metrics across all of them. I'll query errors, throttles, and invocations over the last 24 hours on an hourly basis for each of the roughly 30 functions.

I'm weighing approaches to batch the metric queries efficiently — either individual CloudWatch Metrics Insights calls grouped by function name, or a handful of daily-period queries — and need to settle on epoch-based time parameters since I'm not confident boto3 will accept ISO strings directly.

"}], "parent_id": "000007aa-0000-4000-8000-0000000007aa"}

{"id": "0000088a-0000-4000-8000-00000000088a", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm noticing legacy-runtime gets invoked hourly by EventBridge asynchronously and always fails with no DLQ configured — the 6 invocations likely break down into 2 events with 2 async retries each (3 attempts), matching the failure timestamps around 19:47-19:50, though there could also be a manual invoke mixed in.

The non-zero Errors metric here is notable, so I want to check AsyncEventsDropped/AsyncEventAge and the EventBridge rule's targets and DLQ settings. I should also scan logs for swallowed errors across other active functions (ExampleCleanup, AutoUpdate, exb-dispatcher, example-agent-*, example-publisher, ExdCustomResourceDashboard) over a 7-day window, since list_metrics only surfaced a handful of recently active functions despite more existing with no activity.

"}], "parent_id": "000007aa-0000-4000-8000-0000000007aa"}

{"id": "0000088e-0000-4000-8000-00000000088e", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice the subscriptions list references a topic, "example-obs-devops-agent-alarms," that isn't in the topic list of 3 returned — and there are two duplicate subscriptions to it pointing at the same lambda, suggesting either an orphaned topic or a stale subscription left over after deletion.

Now I'm planning the metrics pull: I'll batch get_metric_data calls across the 4 SQS queues, pulling ApproximateAgeOfOldestMessage (Max), NumberOfMessagesSent/Received (Sum), and ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible (Max) over a 7-day window using daily (86400s) periods to keep the output compact, falling back to hourly resolution only if something looks off.

"}], "parent_id": "000007a0-0000-4000-8000-0000000007a0"}

{"id": "000008ac-0000-4000-8000-0000000008ac", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The apex topic's subscription points to a Lambda function, example-payments-agentcore-webhook, that doesn't appear in the function list — it seems to have been deleted while the subscription remains. I should verify this with get_function, and also check the example-obs-devops-agent-webhook case where a subscription exists for a topic that itself doesn't seem to exist anymore.

I also want to pull invocation and error metrics for all Lambda functions over the past 7 days, likely using a SEARCH-based metric expression with a weekly period to get aggregated sums, plus similar metrics for SQS, DynamoDB, API Gateway, Step Functions, and EventBridge.

"}], "parent_id": "000007a5-0000-4000-8000-0000000007a5"}

{"id": "000008dc-0000-4000-8000-0000000008dc", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice slsreview-legacy-runtime has 6 invocations with 6 errors - a 100% error rate on an hourly schedule, yet there's no alarm configured for it, which seems like a real gap. The other slsreview-* functions look like they were just created recently as a testbed, so this one stands out as having actual production-like traffic.

Beyond this, alarm coverage across the account is thin: only exb-dispatcher, example-publisher, and slsreview-healthy-control have any alarms, and most of those lack real paging actions - the exb and healthy-control alarms have no actions attached, and one alarm even points to an SNS topic subscribed to a deleted Lambda. example-publisher-keyword-publisher's email alarm looks like the only genuinely functioning paging path in the whole setup.

"}], "parent_id": "000007a5-0000-4000-8000-0000000007a5"}

{"id": "000008e6-0000-4000-8000-0000000008e6", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Checking the metrics, I see no failure signals anywhere—Events and SNS have no relevant metrics since there's been no activity in two weeks, DynamoDB shows no throttling or system errors, and the stream consumer shows "No records processed" with an empty on-failure destination while the table itself has zero items.

Other notable gaps: the SQS consumer on no-dlq-queue has no DLQ, no redrive policy, and no ReportBatchItemFailures configured. Looking at the alarms, there's only a DLQ alarm for exb, with nothing covering the slsreview-* queues, API Gateway, Step Functions, or DynamoDB. I'm now pulling HTTP API metrics (Count, 4xx/5xx, latency) over 7 days, plus DynamoDB UserErrors and stream GetRecords, while deciding whether it's worth checking S3 bucket notification configs too.

"}], "parent_id": "000007a0-0000-4000-8000-0000000007a0"}

{"id": "00000949-0000-4000-8000-000000000949", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm spotting a strong correlation: DynamoDB's UserErrors rate of 720/hour works out to exactly 12 per minute, matching exb-dispatcher's 12 failing keys each minute with "resource not found" errors — likely the same ResourceNotFoundException. I'm also noting the exb-dispatcher alarms lack any configured actions, and since the errors metric is sitting at 0, that alarm setup seems moot.

I need to clean up a messy spot in the coverage report around the "10 in ALARM" count and the stale ECS alarms, keep it short, and remember the sub-agent noted slsreview-api has no traffic. No need to ask the user anything — I'll just close with an offer to open a deeper investigation if they want, keeping it in plain prose without exposing internal details.

"}]}
